from . import n
n("minerals_rocks","Minerals and the rock cycle","8",(1e-6,1e5),"C1","Geology",
 "Rocks are aggregates of minerals, which are crystalline solids with defined chemistry. The three rock families (igneous, sedimentary, metamorphic) transform into one another over geological time.",
 "Silicates make up most of the crust; quartz, feldspar and olivine are common. Melting, burial, uplift and erosion cycle the material.",
 "Crust ≈47 % O, 28 % Si, 8 % Al, 5 % Fe by mass; quartz SiO₂ Mohs 7; olivine (Mg,Fe)₂SiO₄; granite ρ≈2.7, basalt ρ≈3.0 g/cm³.",
 "Classification is by origin, not composition alone.",
 em="crystal_lattices chemical_bonding",ev="Petrography|Experimental petrology",refs="AST 8",ex="plate_tectonics weathering_erosion",sim="rock crystal")
n("earth_interior","Earth's interior","9",(1e3,6.4e6),"C1","Geophysics",
 "Earth has a thin crust, a thick convecting rocky mantle, a liquid outer core and a solid inner core. Seismic waves reveal this layering.",
 "Core is mainly iron and nickel. The inner core is solid because of pressure despite temperatures near the Sun's surface.",
 "R=6371 km; crust 5–70 km; mantle to 2891 km; outer core to 5150 km; inner core r≈1220 km; core T≈5000–6000 K; P_center≈364 GPa.",
 "Composition details (light elements in core) uncertain; inner-core dynamics debated.",
 em="gravitation_newton minerals_rocks waves",ev="Seismic tomography|Free oscillations|Meteorite composition",refs="AST 8",ex="plate_tectonics geodynamo seismology",fr="Inner-core dynamics and composition~open",sim="planet:earth")
n("plate_tectonics","Plate tectonics","8",(1e3,1.3e7),"C1","Geology",
 "Earth's rigid outer shell is broken into about 15 plates that drift a few centimetres a year. Plate motions build mountains, open oceans and cause earthquakes and volcanoes.",
 "Plates form at spreading ridges, slide past at transform faults and sink at subduction zones. Mantle convection and slab pull drive them.",
 "Speeds 1–10 cm/yr (Atlantic ≈2.5, Pacific ≈7–10); oceanic crust ≤≈180 Myr; Wilson cycle ≈300–500 Myr.",
 "When plate tectonics started (between ~4 and 2.5 Ga) is debated.",
 em="earth_interior heat_diffusion",ev="Seafloor magnetic stripes|GPS plate motion|Earthquake belts",refs="AST 8",fr="Onset of plate tectonics~open|Role of mantle plumes~open",ex="seismology volcanism mountain_building",sim="planet:earth rock")
n("seismology","Earthquakes and seismic waves","8",(1,1e7),"C1","Geophysics",
 "Earthquakes release energy stored by slowly moving rock when a fault suddenly slips. Seismic waves of several types spread out and are recorded worldwide.",
 "P waves compress, S waves shear (not through liquid) and surface waves cause most damage. Magnitude is logarithmic: each unit means ≈32 times more energy.",
 "log E=1.5M+4.8 (J); M₀=μAD; v_P≈6–8 km/s crust; v_S≈3.5–4.5 km/s; Gutenberg-Richter log N=a−bM, b≈1.",
 "Earthquakes can be forecast only probabilistically.",
 em="plate_tectonics waves",ev="Global seismograph networks",refs="AST 8",fr="Short-term earthquake prediction~open",sim="rock planet:earth")
n("volcanism","Volcanoes and magma","8",(1,1e5),"C1","Geology",
 "Volcanoes erupt where molten rock rises to the surface at plate boundaries and hot spots. Gas content and viscosity decide whether an eruption is gentle or explosive.",
 "Basaltic magma flows easily and forms shields; silica-rich magma is sticky and traps gas, giving explosive eruptions. Eruptions release aerosols that can cool climate temporarily.",
 "Basalt lava ≈1100–1250 °C; Pinatubo 1991 cooled global mean ≈0.5 °C for ≈2 yr; VEI scale logarithmic.",
 "Timing of eruptions is hard to predict.",
 em="plate_tectonics minerals_rocks",ev="Volcano monitoring|Ice-core sulfate",refs="AST 8",sim="rock")
n("mountain_building","Mountains, weathering and erosion","8",(1,1e6),"C1","Geology",
 "Colliding plates push up mountains, while rain, ice, rivers and wind wear them down and carry sediment to the sea. Chemical weathering also removes CO₂ from air.",
 "Uplift and erosion balance over millions of years. Weathering of silicate rocks is a slow thermostat for climate.",
 "Himalaya uplift ≈5 mm/yr; erosion rates 0.01–1 mm/yr; CaSiO₃+CO₂→CaCO₃+SiO₂.",
 "Rates vary with climate and lithology.",
 em="plate_tectonics minerals_rocks",ev="Cosmogenic nuclide dating",refs="AST 8",sim="rock")
n("weathering_erosion","Sedimentary processes and soils","8",(1e-6,1e5),"C1","Geology",
 "Broken-down rock is carried by water and wind, deposited in layers, and cemented into sedimentary rock. Soil forms where rock, air, water and life interact.",
 "Soil layers (horizons) hold nutrients and water that plants rely on. A centimetre of soil can take hundreds of years to form.",
 "Soil formation ≈0.01–0.1 mm/yr; clay <2 μm, silt 2–50 μm, sand 50 μm–2 mm; soil organic C ≈1,500 Pg globally.",
 "Rates vary widely with climate.",
 em="mountain_building minerals_rocks",ev="Soil surveys|Core sampling",refs="AST 8; BIO 31 soil",ex="soil_biology",sim="soil rock")
n("soil_biology","Soil as a living system","8",(1e-6,1),"C1","Ecology",
 "A handful of soil holds billions of microbes, fungi and tiny animals that recycle nutrients and feed plants. Fungal networks connect roots.",
 "Nitrogen-fixing bacteria in root nodules supply plants with ammonia. Soil carbon is a major store.",
 "≈10⁸–10⁹ bacteria per gram; fungal hyphae km per gram; soil pH 4–9.",
 "Complex, hard to model at ecosystem scale.",
 em="weathering_erosion bacteria_archaea",ev="Metagenomics|Mycorrhiza studies",refs="BIO 31",sim="soil mushroom tree")
n("deep_time","Deep time and Earth's history","8",(1e7,1e17),"C1","Geology",
 "Earth is about 4.54 billion years old. Layers of rock and fossils record eras from the Hadean through the Phanerozoic.",
 "Index fossils and radiometric dates build the geologic time scale. Major events include the Moon-forming impact, first life, oxygenation and mass extinctions.",
 "Earth 4.54 Gyr; oldest rocks ≈4.0 Gyr; oldest zircons 4.4 Gyr; Cambrian start 538.8 Ma; Chicxulub 66.0 Ma.",
 "Early-Earth record is sparse.",
 em="radiometric_dating plate_tectonics",ev="Stratigraphy|Meteorite ages",refs="AST 14; AST 8",ex="mass_extinctions",fref="moon_origin earth_water")
n("geodynamo","Earth's magnetic field and the geodynamo","9",(1e3,1e8),"C2","Geophysics",
 "Moving liquid iron in the outer core generates Earth's magnetic field, which shields the surface from charged particles. The field flips polarity at irregular intervals.",
 "Convection driven by heat and crystallisation of the inner core, together with rotation, sustains the dynamo. Auroras are caused by solar particles funnelled along field lines.",
 "Surface field ≈25–65 μT; dipole moment ≈8×10²² A m²; reversals ≈every 0.2–1 Myr (last 780 ka).",
 "Numerical dynamos reproduce key features; detailed mechanisms of reversals are debated.",
 em="earth_interior magnetism_force",ev="Palaeomagnetism|Satellite magnetometry",refs="AST 8",sim="planet:earth")
n("oceans_circulation","Ocean circulation and tides","8",(1e0,1e7),"C1","Oceanography",
 "Ocean water moves in surface gyres driven by wind and in deep currents driven by differences in temperature and saltiness. This circulation carries heat from the tropics to higher latitudes.",
 "Coriolis deflection and continents shape currents like the Gulf Stream. Deep water forms in the North Atlantic and around Antarctica and returns over centuries.",
 "Gulf Stream ≈30 Sv (1 Sv=10⁶ m³/s); AMOC ≈17 Sv; ocean covers 71 % of Earth, mean depth ≈3.7 km; thermohaline turnover ≈1000 yr.",
 "Models resolve large-scale flows; eddy parametrisation is an uncertainty.",
 em="navier_stokes coriolis_effect liquid_water_properties",ev="Argo floats|Satellite altimetry|Hydrographic sections",refs="BIO 44",ex="climate_system amoc_node tides",sim="liquid:water planet:earth")
n("tides","Tides","9",(1e3,1e7),"C1","Oceanography",
 "The Moon's gravity pulls more on the near side of Earth than the far side, stretching the oceans into two bulges. Most coasts see two high tides a day.",
 "The Sun adds a smaller pull, giving spring and neap tides. Tidal friction slowly lengthens Earth's day and pushes the Moon away.",
 "Tidal acceleration ∝GMr/d³; Moon recedes ≈3.8 cm/yr; lunar tide ≈2.2× solar; ocean range 0.5–16 m.",
 "Local tides depend on coast shape and resonance.",
 em="gravitation_newton oceans_circulation",ev="Tide gauges|Lunar laser ranging",refs="AST 4; AST 3",sim="moon planet:earth")
n("coriolis_effect","Coriolis effect","8",(1e3,1e7),"C1","Meteorology",
 "On a rotating Earth, moving air and water curve to the right in the northern hemisphere and to the left in the southern. This makes cyclones spin.",
 "It is an apparent force from viewing motion in a rotating frame. It is negligible in a sink but dominates large weather systems.",
 "a=−2Ω×v; f=2Ω sinφ, Ω=7.292×10⁻⁵ rad/s; Rossby number Ro=U/fL.",
 "Only matters for large scales or long times.",
 em="rotational_dynamics newton_laws",ev="Foucault pendulum|Cyclone rotation",refs="UP1 10",ex="atmospheric_circulation oceans_circulation",sim="planet:earth air")
n("atmosphere_structure","Structure of the atmosphere","9",(1,1e5),"C1","Meteorology",
 "Earth's atmosphere has layers: troposphere (weather), stratosphere (ozone layer), mesosphere and thermosphere. Half the air lies below about 5.5 km.",
 "Temperature falls with height in the troposphere by about 6.5 °C per km, rises in the stratosphere because ozone absorbs UV, and again in the thermosphere.",
 "Troposphere top ≈8–15 km; P(z)=P₀e^{−z/H}, H≈8.4 km; lapse rate Γ≈6.5 K/km (dry adiabatic 9.8 K/km); Kármán line 100 km.",
 "Boundaries vary with latitude and season.",
 em="kinetic_theory gas_air_properties electromagnetic_waves",ev="Radiosondes|Satellite sounders",refs="BIO 44",sim="air cloud")
n("greenhouse_effect","The greenhouse effect","9",(1e0,1e5),"C1","Climate",
 "Gases like water vapour, carbon dioxide and methane let sunlight in but absorb heat leaving the surface, keeping Earth about 33 °C warmer than it would be without them.",
 "Adding more CO₂ reduces outgoing radiation until the planet warms to rebalance. The effect was first described by Fourier, Tyndall and Arrhenius.",
 "T_eff=[S(1−α)/4σ]^{1/4}=255 K; surface 288 K; ΔF≈5.35 ln(C/C₀) W/m² for CO₂; ≈3.7 W/m² per doubling.",
 "Radiative physics is solid; feedbacks (clouds, ice) set the response.",
 em="blackbody atomic_spectra temperature_heat",ev="Satellite spectra of outgoing radiation|Venus surface temperature",refs="AST 10; BIO 44",ex="climate_system",sim="air planet:earth planet:venus")
n("atmospheric_circulation","Weather systems and global circulation","9",(1e3,1e7),"C1","Meteorology",
 "Heating differences between the equator and poles drive large cells of circulation (Hadley, Ferrel, polar) and jet streams. Fronts and storms form where air masses meet.",
 "Rotation bends flow into winds and storms. Water vapour condensation releases heat that fuels thunderstorms and hurricanes.",
 "Hadley cell extends ≈0–30° lat; jet core ≈10–15 km altitude, speeds ≈50–100 m/s; hurricane wind ≥33 m/s.",
 "Chaotic: small errors grow, limiting detailed forecasts to roughly 2 weeks.",
 em="navier_stokes coriolis_effect temperature_heat",ev="Reanalysis datasets|Radiosonde networks",refs="BIO 44",ex="weather_prediction hurricanes_storms",sim="cloud air planet:earth")
n("weather_dynamics","Weather and clouds","9",(1,1e6),"C1","Meteorology",
 "Weather arises when air rises, cools and its water vapour condenses into clouds and precipitation. Warm, moist air carries the energy.",
 "Cloud droplets (≈10 μm) need condensation nuclei like dust and sea salt. They grow by collision into raindrops about 1 mm across.",
 "Latent heat L_v=2.5 MJ/kg; Clausius-Clapeyron: saturation vapour pressure rises ≈7 %/K; cloud droplet ≈10 μm, raindrop ≈1 mm.",
 "Microphysics parameterised in models.",
 em="atmospheric_circulation turbulence phase_transitions",ev="Radar and satellite meteorology",refs="BIO 44",sim="cloud air")
n("weather_prediction","Weather forecasting","9",(1e3,1e7),"C2","Meteorology",
 "Forecasts run physical equations on computers starting from observations. Skill reaches about 10 days for large systems, limited by chaos.",
 "Machine-learning models trained on past weather now rival physics-based ones for some measures. Ensembles show uncertainty.",
 "Lorenz limit ≈2 weeks; 5-day forecast skill ≈ today's 1-day skill of the 1980s; grid ≈9 km (ECMWF HRES).",
 "Extreme events and local convection remain hard.",
 em="atmospheric_circulation dynamical_chaos",ev="Forecast verification statistics",refs="DSC 5")
n("hurricanes_storms","Tropical cyclones and severe storms","9",(1e2,1e6),"C1","Meteorology",
 "Hurricanes draw energy from warm ocean water (above ~26.5 °C) and spin because of Earth's rotation. Thunderstorms form when warm moist air rises rapidly.",
 "Wind strength, rain and storm surge cause damage. Warmer seas favour more intense storms, though trends in frequency are uncertain.",
 "Wind ≥119 km/h for hurricane; power ≈10¹⁴ W (latent heating); storm surge up to ≈8 m; SST threshold ≈26.5 °C.",
 "Link to climate change is robust for intensity, less certain for frequency.",
 em="atmospheric_circulation weather_dynamics",ev="Best-track records|Satellite imagery",refs="BIO 44",sim="cloud")
n("water_cycle","The water cycle","8",(1,1e7),"C1","Hydrology",
 "Water evaporates, condenses into clouds, falls as rain or snow, and flows back to the sea. Sunlight drives the cycle, and nearly all of Earth's water is in the oceans.",
 "Residence times range from days in the atmosphere to thousands of years in deep ice and groundwater.",
 "Oceans 97 %, ice ≈2 %, fresh liquid <1 %; evaporation ≈5×10⁵ km³/yr; atmosphere holds ≈1.3×10⁴ km³ (≈9-day residence).",
 "Human withdrawals alter flows locally.",
 em="liquid_water_properties temperature_heat",ev="Gauging stations|GRACE satellite",refs="BIO 44; BIO 46",ex="climate_system",fref="earth_water",sim="pond cloud")
n("biogeochemical_cycles","Carbon, nitrogen and phosphorus cycles","8",(1,1e7),"C1","Earth system",
 "Elements circulate between rock, ocean, air and living things. These cycles connect geology to biology and set climate over long times.",
 "Carbon moves between air, oceans, plants and rocks; nitrogen is fixed by microbes and human industry; phosphorus comes mainly from rock weathering and limits growth in many places.",
 "Atmospheric C ≈ 850 Pg; ocean ≈38 000 Pg; land plants ≈450–650 Pg; fossil-fuel emissions ≈ 10 Pg C/yr in recent decades; N fixation by industry ≈ natural land fixation.",
 "Fluxes uncertain by ~10–20 %.",
 em="ecology_energy bacteria_archaea mountain_building",ev="Isotope tracers|Atmospheric sampling",refs="BIO 46",ex="carbon_cycle_climate nitrogen_cycle")
n("nitrogen_cycle","The nitrogen cycle","8",(1,1e7),"C1","Earth system",
 "Nitrogen gas is turned into usable ammonia by bacteria and lightning, then cycles through plants and animals and is returned to air by denitrifying microbes.",
 "Fertiliser runoff causes eutrophication. Humans have roughly doubled the amount of reactive nitrogen entering ecosystems.",
 "N fixation: natural ≈110, industrial ≈ 120 Tg N/yr; nitrogenase requires 16 ATP per N₂.",
 "Budgets uncertain.",
 em="biogeochemical_cycles haber_bosch",ev="¹⁵N tracers",refs="BIO 46; MIC 8",sim="soil")
n("carbon_cycle_climate","The carbon cycle and CO₂ in the atmosphere","8",(1,1e7),"C1","Climate",
 "Carbon dioxide is exchanged between air, oceans and plants. Burning fossil fuels and clearing forests has raised atmospheric CO₂ far above the pre-industrial level of about 280 ppm.",
 "About half of emitted CO₂ stays in the air; the rest is absorbed by ocean and land. The seasonal sawtooth in the Mauna Loa record reflects Northern Hemisphere plant growth.",
 "Pre-industrial ≈280 ppm; Mauna Loa passed 400 ppm in 2013; ≈2.1 Pg C per ppm; airborne fraction ≈0.45; isotope δ¹³C shows fossil origin.",
 "Future sinks depend on warming, ocean chemistry and land use.",
 em="biogeochemical_cycles greenhouse_effect",ev="Keeling curve|Ice-core CO₂ (800 kyr)|¹⁴C Suess effect",refs="BIO 44",fref="co2_ppm",ex="ocean_acidification climate_system")
n("ocean_acidification","Ocean acidification","8",(1e-6,1e7),"C1","Climate",
 "Seawater absorbs CO₂, which forms carbonic acid and lowers pH. Since the pre-industrial era surface pH has fallen by about 0.1 units, roughly a 30 % rise in acidity.",
 "More acidity makes it harder for corals, shellfish and some plankton to build calcium-carbonate shells.",
 "CO₂+H₂O⇌H₂CO₃⇌H⁺+HCO₃⁻; pH 8.2→8.1; [H⁺] +≈30 %; saturation state Ω=[Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻]/K_sp.",
 "Buffering varies regionally.",
 em="carbon_cycle_climate chemistry_equilibrium",ev="Time-series stations (HOT, BATS)",refs="CHE 14; BIO 44")
n("climate_system","The climate system and global warming","9",(1e3,1e7),"C1","Climate",
 "Earth's climate is set by sunlight, atmosphere, oceans, ice and life. Human activity has unequivocally warmed the planet by about 1.1 °C since the late 1800s.",
 "Energy imbalance mostly goes into the oceans (about 90 %). Feedbacks like melting ice and water vapour amplify warming, while aerosols cool a little.",
 "Radiative forcing F; ΔT=λF with equilibrium sensitivity λ; Earth energy imbalance ≈0.5–1 W/m²; sea-level rise ≈3–4 mm/yr recently.",
 "Physics solid; regional details and tipping thresholds uncertain.",
 em="greenhouse_effect carbon_cycle_climate oceans_circulation water_cycle",ev="Instrumental records|Ocean heat content|Attribution studies",refs="BIO 44",fref="co2_ppm climate_sensitivity",
 fr="Tipping elements (ice sheets, Amazon, permafrost, reefs)~open|Aerosol forcing and pattern effect~open",ex="milankovitch ice_sheets amoc_node")
n("milankovitch","Ice ages and orbital cycles","9",(1e6,1e17),"C1","Climate",
 "Slow changes in Earth's orbit and tilt alter how sunlight is spread over latitude and season, pacing the ice ages. Feedbacks from CO₂ and ice amplify these small changes.",
 "Cycles of about 100, 41 and 21 thousand years appear in ocean sediments and ice cores.",
 "Eccentricity ≈100 & 400 kyr; obliquity 22.1–24.5°, 41 kyr; precession ≈19–23 kyr; glacial CO₂ ≈180 vs interglacial ≈280 ppm.",
 "Why the 100-kyr cycle dominates in the last million years is still debated.",
 em="orbits_kepler climate_system",ev="Marine δ¹⁸O records|Vostok and EPICA ice cores",refs="AST 4")
n("ice_sheets","Ice sheets and sea level","9",(1e3,1e7),"C1","Climate",
 "Ice sheets in Greenland and Antarctica hold enough water to raise sea level by about 65 metres if fully melted. Glaciers flow slowly under their own weight.",
 "Warming ocean water can undercut floating ice shelves, speeding up flow. Sea level also rises because water expands when it warms.",
 "Greenland ≈7.4 m, West Antarctic ≈5 m, East Antarctic ≈53 m SLE; ice flow v ∝ τ³ (Glen's law n≈3).",
 "Rate of future ice loss is the largest uncertainty in sea-level projections.",
 em="climate_system fluid_statics",ev="GRACE|Satellite altimetry|Ice cores",refs="BIO 44",fr="Rate of future ice-sheet loss~open",sim="rock")
n("amoc_node","Atlantic overturning circulation (AMOC)","9",(1e5,1e7),"C3","Climate",
 "The Atlantic overturning circulation brings warm water north and sends cold deep water south. It moderates the climate of northwestern Europe.",
 "Freshwater from melting ice could weaken it. How close it is to a tipping point is debated.",
 "≈17 Sv at 26.5°N; heat transport ≈1.2 PW; Stommel bistable model.",
 "Short observational record limits conclusions.",
 em="oceans_circulation climate_system",ev="RAPID array at 26.5°N|Paleoclimate abrupt events",refs="BIO 44",fref="amoc",fr="Timing and reversibility of any collapse~open")
n("ozone_layer","The ozone layer","9",(1e4,5e4),"C1","Atmospheric chemistry",
 "A thin layer of ozone in the stratosphere absorbs most harmful ultraviolet light. Industrial chlorofluorocarbons broke it down, creating the Antarctic ozone hole.",
 "The Montreal Protocol phased out these chemicals and the layer is slowly recovering.",
 "O₃ peak ≈ 20–25 km; column ≈300 DU (Dobson units); O₃+hν(<310 nm); Cl catalytic cycle: Cl+O₃→ClO+O₂.",
 "Recovery expected over decades.",
 em="atmosphere_structure reaction_kinetics",ev="Ozone hole observations 1985|Montreal Protocol results",refs="CHE 12",sim="air")
